{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ek5qkeLJZQUhtg4FiqNbLo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WiLXaV8GgdZUJoWcqyrJni","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research stacked reduction code","prompt":"I'm investigating performance bottlenecks in the stacked reduction component of a GPU prover (CUDA backend for a STARK proof system). The stacked reduction is an \"openings\" phase that uses sumcheck.\n\nThe key issue: at APC 300, the `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel` has 10,348 tiny kernel launches (avg 3.9μs each), and the wall-clock time for Stacked Reduction is 310ms while GPU kernel time is only ~93ms. The CPU-side overhead of launching these tiny kernels is the bottleneck.\n\nI need you to find and read the following:\n\n1. The stacked reduction CUDA implementation in the cuda-backend crate — find the main file(s) that orchestrate the stacked reduction sumcheck. Look in `crates/cuda-backend/` for files related to \"stacked_reduction\" or \"openings\".\n\n2. The kernel definitions for:\n   - `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`\n   - `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_kernel`\n   - `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel`\n   - `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`\n\n3. The CPU-side Rust code that launches these kernels in a loop. I need to understand:\n   - How the loop over AIR instances is structured\n   - Where synchronization/blocking happens\n   - Whether kernels are launched one-at-a-time or batched\n   - What CPU-side work happens between kernel launches\n\nPlease read the relevant source files in full and report:\n- File paths and line numbers for the key functions\n- The loop structure that launches the degenerate kernels\n- Any obvious batching opportunities (could multiple small kernel launches be merged into one?)\n- The data dependencies between consecutive kernel launches\n\nThis is research only — do not modify any code.","s