{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WiLXaV8GgdZUJoWcqyrJni","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information needed. Let me create a comprehensive report.\n\n## Analysis Report: Stacked Reduction CUDA Kernel Bottleneck\n\n### Executive Summary\n\nThe performance bottleneck in the stacked reduction component is caused by **launching many tiny kernels one-at-a-time** through a CPU loop over \"windows\" (height-grouped columns). At APC 300, approximately **10,348 degenerate kernel launches** execute in sequence, each averaging only **3.9μs of actual GPU kernel execution time**. The CPU overhead of launching these kernels dominates: **310ms wall-clock time vs 93ms GPU kernel time** indicates roughly **217ms (70%) is CPU-side overhead**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Key File Locations\n\n**Rust Orchestration:**\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` — Main prover logic\n  - Function: `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` — Lines 793-912 (contains the kernel launch loop)\n  - Function: `prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu()` — Lines 185-270 (entry point)\n  \n**CUDA Kernel Definitions:**\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel` — Lines 306-368\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_kernel` — Lines 228-302 (non-degenerate case)\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` — Lines 87-153\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` — Lines 158-204\n  - Launcher: `_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate()` — Lines 537-570\n\n**CUDA Wrapper (FFI):**\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n  - Wrapper: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate()` — Lines 245-273\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Critical Loop Structure\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, Lines 833-907\n\n```rust\n// Main loop over \"windows\" (height-grouped column ranges)\nfor window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2) {\n    let window_len = window[1] - window[0];  // Number of columns in this window\n    \n    // Pointer arithmetic to get pointers to this window's data\n    let unstacked_cols_ptr = unsafe { self.d_unstacked_cols.as_ptr().add(window[0]) };\n    let lambda_pows_ptr = unsafe { self.d_lambda_pows.as_ptr().add(2 * window[0]) };\n    \n    let log_height = self.unstacked_cols[window[0]].log_height as usize;\n    \n    // Zero-initialize accumulator (D2H copy)\n    self.d_accum.fill_zero()?;\n    \n    // ===== DECISION POINT: Degenerate vs Non-Degenerate =====\n    if log_height < l_skip + round {\n        // DEGENERATE CASE: Eq and k_rot values are stable (constants for this round)\n        let eq_r = self.eq_stable[log_height];\n        let k_rot_r = self.k_rot_stable[log_height];\n        \n        // Copy eq_ub values to device for this window\n        let eq_ub_slice = &self.eq_ub_per_trace[window[0]..window[1]];\n        if eq_ub_slice.len() > self.d_eq_ub.len() {\n            self.d_eq_ub = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(eq_ub_slice.len());\n        }\n        eq_ub_slice.copy_to(&mut self.d_eq_ub)?;  // H2D copy\n        \n        // ===== KERNEL LAUNCH: ONE PER WINDOW =====\n        unsafe {\n            stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate(\n                &self.d_q_eval_ptrs,          // Pointers to Q matrices\n                &self.d_eq_ub,                // Eq_ub values (window_len elements)\n                eq_r,                          // Scalar constant\n                k_rot_r,                       // Scalar constant\n                unstacked_cols_ptr,            // Start of window columns\n                lambda_pows_ptr,               // Start of window lambda powers\n                &mut self.d_accum,             // Output accumulator\n                stacked_height,                // Q matrix height\n                window_len,                    // Number of columns in window\n                l_skip,\n                round,\n            )?;\n        }\n    } else {\n        // NON-DEGENERATE CASE: Different kernel with different computation\n        // [Similar structure, different kernel call]\n    }\n    \n    // ===== DATA TRANSFER BACK =====\n    let h_accum = self.d_accum.to_host()?;  // D2H copy\n    let evals = reduce_raw_u64_to_ef(&h_accum);\n    s_evals_batch.push(evals);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Kernel Implementation Details\n\n#### Degenerate Kernel (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`)\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`, Lines 306-368\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel(\n    const FpExt * const * q_evals,       // Array of pointers to Q matrices\n    const FpExt *eq_ub_ptr,              // Pre-computed eq(u, b) per column\n    FpExt eq_r,                          // Eq(r[1..n], H_n) - CONSTANT scalar\n    FpExt k_rot_r,                       // K_rot(r[1..n], H_n) - CONSTANT scalar\n    const UnstackedSlice *unstacked_cols, // Column descriptors\n    const FpExt *lambda_pows,            // Batching randomness powers\n    uint64_t *output,                    // u64 atomic accumulators\n    uint32_t q_height,\n    uint32_t window_len,\n    uint32_t shift_factor                // = l_skip + round\n) {\n    // Thread loop: each thread handles multiple window indices\n    for (uint32_t window_idx = threadIdx.x; window_idx < window_len; window_idx += blockDim.x) {\n        UnstackedSlice s = unstacked_cols[window_idx];\n        \n        // Load Q matrix reference for this column\n        const FpExt *q = q_evals[s.commit_idx];\n        \n        // Index calculation\n        uint32_t col_idx = s.stacked_col_idx;\n        uint32_t row_idx = s.stacked_row_idx;\n        uint32_t row_start = (row_idx >> shift_factor) << 1;  // Get 2 consecutive rows\n        uint32_t q_offset = col_idx * q_height + row_start;\n        \n        // Load Q values (linear interpolation)\n        FpExt q_0 = q[q_offset];\n        FpExt q_1 = q[q_offset + 1];\n        FpExt q_c1 = q_1 - q_0;\n        \n        // Extract bit b from stacked row index\n        uint32_t b_bool = (row_idx >> (shift_factor - 1)) & 1;\n        \n        // Sumcheck round evaluation (unrolled loop for S_DEG=2)\n        for (int x_int = 1; x_int <= S_DEG; ++x_int) {\n            // Interpolate Q at x = x_int\n            FpExt q_x = q_0 + q_c1 * x;\n            \n            // Compute eq(u_b, x, b) where u_b is the eq_ub value\n            FpExt eq_ub_x = eq_ub[window_idx] * eq1(x, b);  // eq1(x,b) = 1 - x - b + 2xb\n            \n            // Final contributions\n            FpExt eq = eq_r * eq_ub_x;\n            FpExt k_rot = k_rot_r * eq_ub_x;\n            \n            // Weighted accumulation\n            local_sums[x_int - 1] +=\n                (lambda_pows[2 * window_idx] * eq + lambda_pows[2 * window_idx + 1] * k_rot) * q_x;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Reduce within block using warp-aggregated atomics\n    for (int idx = 0; idx < S_DEG; idx++) {\n        FpExt reduced = block_reduce_sum(local_sums[idx], shared);\n        if (threadIdx.x == 0) {\n            atomic_add_fpext_to_u64(output + idx * 4, reduced);\n        }\n        __syncthreads();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Launch Configuration:**\n```cuda\ndim3 block(std::min(window_len, 256u));  // 1D block, size = min(window_len, 256)\ndim3 grid(1);                            // Single block (!)\nsize_t shmem_bytes = div_ceil(block.x, WARP_SIZE) * sizeof(FpExt);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Bottleneck Analysis\n\n#### CPU-Side Overhead per Window\n\nFor each window (height-group):\n\n1. **`d_accum.fill_zero()`** — Kernel launch for zeroing (~0.5-1μs)\n2. **`eq_ub_slice.copy_to(&mut d_eq_ub)`** — H2D memory copy (~1-2μs for typical window_len)\n3. **Degenerate kernel launch** — The actual computation kernel (~3-5μs typical)\n4. **`d_accum.to_host()`** — D2H memory copy + GPU sync (~1-2μs)\n\n**Total per window:** ~6-10μs of overhead, but only ~3-5μs of actual kernel work.\n\n#### At APC 300\n\n- **Number of windows:** ~10,348 (implies many very small AIR instances or many columns grouped separately)\n- **Total wall-clock time:** 310ms\n- **GPU kernel execution time:** 93ms\n- **CPU overhead time:** ~217ms (70% of total)\n\n**Implication:** Launching ~10k tiny kernels sequentially creates massive scheduler/driver overhead.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Data Dependencies Between Launches\n\n**Per-window data flow:**\n\n```\nInput (Constant across window):\n├── eq_stable[log_height]            // Read from CPU memory (stable array)\n├── k_rot_stable[log_height]         // Read from CPU memory (stable array)\n├── d_q_eval_ptrs                    // Device pointers (same for all windows)\n└── d_unstacked_cols + window[0]     // Device pointer offset per window\n\nPer-window H2D Transfer:\n└── eq_ub_per_trace[window[0]..window[1]] → d_eq_ub\n\nKernel Launch:\n└── stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate(...)\n\nPer-window D2H Transfer:\n└── d_accum → h_accum\n\nOutput:\n└── s_evals_batch.push(evals)\n```\n\n**Key observation:** **Windows are independent**. The kernel launch for window `i` has no data dependency on the output of window `i-1`. The only dependency is on GPU sync (implicit in H2D copies).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Batching Opportunities\n\n#### Problem: One Kernel Launch Per Window\n\nCurrently, the structure is:\n```\nFor each window i:\n  Fill zero d_accum\n  H2D copy eq_ub[window_i]\n  Launch kernel (grid=1, block=min(window_len, 256))\n  D2H copy d_accum\n```\n\n#### Opportunity 1: Batch Multiple Windows into Single Kernel\n\n**Idea:** Launch a single 2D kernel with multiple windows in the Y dimension:\n\n```cuda\n// Pseudocode\nfor window_idx = 0..num_windows:\n  for window_block_y = window_idx:\n    for thread_x in block:\n      Compute contribution for columns in window_block_y\n      Atomic accumulate into output[window_block_y]\n```\n\n**Changes needed:**\n1. Modify degenerate kernel to handle multiple windows per launch\n2. Pre-allocate accumulators for all windows (or all windows of same height)\n3. Change grid from `grid(1)` to `grid(1, num_windows)` or similar\n4. Single H2D copy for all eq_ub values\n\n**Expected benefit:** Replace 10,348 launches with 1-2 launches per height group (or fewer), reducing CPU overhead by 1-2 orders of magnitude.\n\n#### Opportunity 2: Fuse Fill-Zero with First Kernel\n\nCurrently, `fill_zero()` launches a separate kernel. For atomic accumulators that are reused, this could be fused into the main kernel with proper initialization.\n\n#### Opportunity 3: Batch H2D Copies\n\nInstead of copying eq_ub per window, batch all eq_ub copies for a height group before the kernel launch.\n\n#### Opportunity 4: Async H2D/D2H with Kernel Overlap\n\nUse CUDA streams and async copies to overlap:\n- H2D for window i+1\n- Kernel execution for window i\n- D2H for window i (from previous iteration)\n\n---\n\n### 7. Non-Degenerate Kernel Path\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`, Lines 228-302\n\nThis path is taken when `log_height >= l_skip + round`. It has:\n- Multiple threads processing Y-dimension coordinates\n- Windowed striding in the X dimension\n- Automatic tuning of `grid.y` (window stride) based on SM count\n\n**Launch configuration:**\n```cuda\nauto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(num_y, 256);\n// Auto-tuned window stride (grid.y) based on occupancy targets\nuint32_t tuned_stride = ...;\ngrid.y = tuned_stride;\n```\n\nThis kernel is more flexible for larger problems but still has the same per-window launch overhead.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Summary: Data Dependencies and Merging Feasibility\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Window Independence** | **Yes** — Each window accesses disjoint column ranges. Zero data dependency between windows. |\n| **Scalars (eq_r, k_rot_r)** | Same per height-group. Could broadcast to all windows of same height in one kernel. |\n| **Q matrices** | Pre-computed, static. Passed as device pointers (already optimal). |\n| **eq_ub values** | Per-column, varies per window. Could batch H2D or pre-compute. |\n| **Accumulators** | Currently one per window. Could use 2D array `accum[height_group][S_DEG * D_EF]`. |\n| **Merging Feasibility** | **High** — 10,348 launches could likely reduce to ~100-1000 launches by grouping windows by height. |\n\n---\n\n### 9. Code Flow Summary\n\n**Main entry point:** `prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu()` (Line 185)\n\n**Round 0:** `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly()` (Line 484)\n- Calls: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0()` kernel once per trace\n- Round 0 does not use degenerate kernels\n\n**Rounds 1..n_stack:** `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` (Line 793) — **THE BOTTLENECK**\n- Loop over `ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)` — One iteration per height-group (window)\n- Decision: degenerate or non-degenerate path based on `log_height < l_skip + round`\n- **Degenerate path (lines 848-878):** Launches one tiny kernel per window\n  - This is where 10,348 launches occur at APC 300\n- Each window launch includes H2D/D2H copies around the kernel\n\n**Folding:** `fold_mle_evals()` (Line 914)\n- Folds Q matrices and eq/k_rot polynomials between rounds\n\n---\n\n### 10. Visualization of the Problem\n\n```\nTimeline at APC 300 (simplified, 10,348 windows):\n\nRound 1:\n  Window 0: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┐\n  Window 1: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┼─ 310ms wall-clock\n  Window 2: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┤   93ms actual GPU work\n  ...\n  Window 10347: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┘\n                     ↑                        ↑                  ↑\n                Overhead              Overhead (3.9μs avg)    Overhead\n\nPotential Optimization:\n\nRound 1:\n  Batch H2D(all eq_ub) ──┐\n  Launch kernel(N_windows) with grid.y=N_windows ──┼─ ~10-30ms\n  Batch D2H(all accum) ──┘\n                    ↑ 10-100x speedup in CPU overhead\n```\n\n---\n\n### Files Summary\n\n| File Path | Key Content | Lines |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` | Orchestration, kernel launch loop | 793-912 |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` | Degenerate kernel def | 306-368 |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` | Degenerate kernel launcher | 537-570 |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs` | Rust FFI wrapper | 245-273 |"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8b3f9a5-d318-4292-b8d9-cc11d3b0ac84","uuid":"a070daff-15d4-4b92-abe2-1fc2291b026b","timestamp":"2026-04-10T18:04:56.600Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm investigating performance bottlenecks in the stacked reduction component of a GPU prover (CUDA backend for a STARK proof system). The stacked reduction is an \"openings\" phase that uses sumcheck.\n\nThe key issue: at APC 300, the `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel` has 10,348 tiny kernel launches (avg 3.9μs each), and the wall-clock time for Stacked Reduction is 310ms while GPU kernel time is only ~93ms. The CPU-side overhead of launching these tiny kernels is the bottleneck.\n\nI need you to find and read the following:\n\n1. The stacked reduction CUDA implementation in the cuda-backend crate — find the main file(s) that orchestrate the stacked reduction sumcheck. Look in `crates/cuda-backend/` for files related to \"stacked_reduction\" or \"openings\".\n\n2. The kernel definitions for:\n   - `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`\n   - `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_kernel`\n   - `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel`\n   - `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`\n\n3. The CPU-side Rust code that launches these kernels in a loop. I need to understand:\n   - How the loop over AIR instances is structured\n   - Where synchronization/blocking happens\n   - Whether kernels are launched one-at-a-time or batched\n   - What CPU-side work happens between kernel launches\n\nPlease read the relevant source files in full and report:\n- File paths and line numbers for the key functions\n- The loop structure that launches the degenerate kernels\n- Any obvious batching opportunities (could multiple small kernel launches be merged into one?)\n- The data dependencies between consecutive kernel launches\n\nThis is research only — do not modify any code.","agentId":"a9e198b9895e55c9d","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information needed. Let me create a comprehensive report.\n\n## Analysis Report: Stacked Reduction CUDA Kernel Bottleneck\n\n### Executive Summary\n\nThe performance bottleneck in the stacked reduction component is caused by **launching many tiny kernels one-at-a-time** through a CPU loop over \"windows\" (height-grouped columns). At APC 300, approximately **10,348 degenerate kernel launches** execute in sequence, each averaging only **3.9μs of actual GPU kernel execution time**. The CPU overhead of launching these kernels dominates: **310ms wall-clock time vs 93ms GPU kernel time** indicates roughly **217ms (70%) is CPU-side overhead**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Key File Locations\n\n**Rust Orchestration:**\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` — Main prover logic\n  - Function: `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` — Lines 793-912 (contains the kernel launch loop)\n  - Function: `prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu()` — Lines 185-270 (entry point)\n  \n**CUDA Kernel Definitions:**\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel` — Lines 306-368\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_kernel` — Lines 228-302 (non-degenerate case)\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` — Lines 87-153\n  - Kernel: `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` — Lines 158-204\n  - Launcher: `_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate()` — Lines 537-570\n\n**CUDA Wrapper (FFI):**\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n  - Wrapper: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate()` — Lines 245-273\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Critical Loop Structure\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, Lines 833-907\n\n```rust\n// Main loop over \"windows\" (height-grouped column ranges)\nfor window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2) {\n    let window_len = window[1] - window[0];  // Number of columns in this window\n    \n    // Pointer arithmetic to get pointers to this window's data\n    let unstacked_cols_ptr = unsafe { self.d_unstacked_cols.as_ptr().add(window[0]) };\n    let lambda_pows_ptr = unsafe { self.d_lambda_pows.as_ptr().add(2 * window[0]) };\n    \n    let log_height = self.unstacked_cols[window[0]].log_height as usize;\n    \n    // Zero-initialize accumulator (D2H copy)\n    self.d_accum.fill_zero()?;\n    \n    // ===== DECISION POINT: Degenerate vs Non-Degenerate =====\n    if log_height < l_skip + round {\n        // DEGENERATE CASE: Eq and k_rot values are stable (constants for this round)\n        let eq_r = self.eq_stable[log_height];\n        let k_rot_r = self.k_rot_stable[log_height];\n        \n        // Copy eq_ub values to device for this window\n        let eq_ub_slice = &self.eq_ub_per_trace[window[0]..window[1]];\n        if eq_ub_slice.len() > self.d_eq_ub.len() {\n            self.d_eq_ub = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(eq_ub_slice.len());\n        }\n        eq_ub_slice.copy_to(&mut self.d_eq_ub)?;  // H2D copy\n        \n        // ===== KERNEL LAUNCH: ONE PER WINDOW =====\n        unsafe {\n            stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate(\n                &self.d_q_eval_ptrs,          // Pointers to Q matrices\n                &self.d_eq_ub,                // Eq_ub values (window_len elements)\n                eq_r,                          // Scalar constant\n                k_rot_r,                       // Scalar constant\n                unstacked_cols_ptr,            // Start of window columns\n                lambda_pows_ptr,               // Start of window lambda powers\n                &mut self.d_accum,             // Output accumulator\n                stacked_height,                // Q matrix height\n                window_len,                    // Number of columns in window\n                l_skip,\n                round,\n            )?;\n        }\n    } else {\n        // NON-DEGENERATE CASE: Different kernel with different computation\n        // [Similar structure, different kernel call]\n    }\n    \n    // ===== DATA TRANSFER BACK =====\n    let h_accum = self.d_accum.to_host()?;  // D2H copy\n    let evals = reduce_raw_u64_to_ef(&h_accum);\n    s_evals_batch.push(evals);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Kernel Implementation Details\n\n#### Degenerate Kernel (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`)\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`, Lines 306-368\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel(\n    const FpExt * const * q_evals,       // Array of pointers to Q matrices\n    const FpExt *eq_ub_ptr,              // Pre-computed eq(u, b) per column\n    FpExt eq_r,                          // Eq(r[1..n], H_n) - CONSTANT scalar\n    FpExt k_rot_r,                       // K_rot(r[1..n], H_n) - CONSTANT scalar\n    const UnstackedSlice *unstacked_cols, // Column descriptors\n    const FpExt *lambda_pows,            // Batching randomness powers\n    uint64_t *output,                    // u64 atomic accumulators\n    uint32_t q_height,\n    uint32_t window_len,\n    uint32_t shift_factor                // = l_skip + round\n) {\n    // Thread loop: each thread handles multiple window indices\n    for (uint32_t window_idx = threadIdx.x; window_idx < window_len; window_idx += blockDim.x) {\n        UnstackedSlice s = unstacked_cols[window_idx];\n        \n        // Load Q matrix reference for this column\n        const FpExt *q = q_evals[s.commit_idx];\n        \n        // Index calculation\n        uint32_t col_idx = s.stacked_col_idx;\n        uint32_t row_idx = s.stacked_row_idx;\n        uint32_t row_start = (row_idx >> shift_factor) << 1;  // Get 2 consecutive rows\n        uint32_t q_offset = col_idx * q_height + row_start;\n        \n        // Load Q values (linear interpolation)\n        FpExt q_0 = q[q_offset];\n        FpExt q_1 = q[q_offset + 1];\n        FpExt q_c1 = q_1 - q_0;\n        \n        // Extract bit b from stacked row index\n        uint32_t b_bool = (row_idx >> (shift_factor - 1)) & 1;\n        \n        // Sumcheck round evaluation (unrolled loop for S_DEG=2)\n        for (int x_int = 1; x_int <= S_DEG; ++x_int) {\n            // Interpolate Q at x = x_int\n            FpExt q_x = q_0 + q_c1 * x;\n            \n            // Compute eq(u_b, x, b) where u_b is the eq_ub value\n            FpExt eq_ub_x = eq_ub[window_idx] * eq1(x, b);  // eq1(x,b) = 1 - x - b + 2xb\n            \n            // Final contributions\n            FpExt eq = eq_r * eq_ub_x;\n            FpExt k_rot = k_rot_r * eq_ub_x;\n            \n            // Weighted accumulation\n            local_sums[x_int - 1] +=\n                (lambda_pows[2 * window_idx] * eq + lambda_pows[2 * window_idx + 1] * k_rot) * q_x;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Reduce within block using warp-aggregated atomics\n    for (int idx = 0; idx < S_DEG; idx++) {\n        FpExt reduced = block_reduce_sum(local_sums[idx], shared);\n        if (threadIdx.x == 0) {\n            atomic_add_fpext_to_u64(output + idx * 4, reduced);\n        }\n        __syncthreads();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Launch Configuration:**\n```cuda\ndim3 block(std::min(window_len, 256u));  // 1D block, size = min(window_len, 256)\ndim3 grid(1);                            // Single block (!)\nsize_t shmem_bytes = div_ceil(block.x, WARP_SIZE) * sizeof(FpExt);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Bottleneck Analysis\n\n#### CPU-Side Overhead per Window\n\nFor each window (height-group):\n\n1. **`d_accum.fill_zero()`** — Kernel launch for zeroing (~0.5-1μs)\n2. **`eq_ub_slice.copy_to(&mut d_eq_ub)`** — H2D memory copy (~1-2μs for typical window_len)\n3. **Degenerate kernel launch** — The actual computation kernel (~3-5μs typical)\n4. **`d_accum.to_host()`** — D2H memory copy + GPU sync (~1-2μs)\n\n**Total per window:** ~6-10μs of overhead, but only ~3-5μs of actual kernel work.\n\n#### At APC 300\n\n- **Number of windows:** ~10,348 (implies many very small AIR instances or many columns grouped separately)\n- **Total wall-clock time:** 310ms\n- **GPU kernel execution time:** 93ms\n- **CPU overhead time:** ~217ms (70% of total)\n\n**Implication:** Launching ~10k tiny kernels sequentially creates massive scheduler/driver overhead.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Data Dependencies Between Launches\n\n**Per-window data flow:**\n\n```\nInput (Constant across window):\n├── eq_stable[log_height]            // Read from CPU memory (stable array)\n├── k_rot_stable[log_height]         // Read from CPU memory (stable array)\n├── d_q_eval_ptrs                    // Device pointers (same for all windows)\n└── d_unstacked_cols + window[0]     // Device pointer offset per window\n\nPer-window H2D Transfer:\n└── eq_ub_per_trace[window[0]..window[1]] → d_eq_ub\n\nKernel Launch:\n└── stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate(...)\n\nPer-window D2H Transfer:\n└── d_accum → h_accum\n\nOutput:\n└── s_evals_batch.push(evals)\n```\n\n**Key observation:** **Windows are independent**. The kernel launch for window `i` has no data dependency on the output of window `i-1`. The only dependency is on GPU sync (implicit in H2D copies).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Batching Opportunities\n\n#### Problem: One Kernel Launch Per Window\n\nCurrently, the structure is:\n```\nFor each window i:\n  Fill zero d_accum\n  H2D copy eq_ub[window_i]\n  Launch kernel (grid=1, block=min(window_len, 256))\n  D2H copy d_accum\n```\n\n#### Opportunity 1: Batch Multiple Windows into Single Kernel\n\n**Idea:** Launch a single 2D kernel with multiple windows in the Y dimension:\n\n```cuda\n// Pseudocode\nfor window_idx = 0..num_windows:\n  for window_block_y = window_idx:\n    for thread_x in block:\n      Compute contribution for columns in window_block_y\n      Atomic accumulate into output[window_block_y]\n```\n\n**Changes needed:**\n1. Modify degenerate kernel to handle multiple windows per launch\n2. Pre-allocate accumulators for all windows (or all windows of same height)\n3. Change grid from `grid(1)` to `grid(1, num_windows)` or similar\n4. Single H2D copy for all eq_ub values\n\n**Expected benefit:** Replace 10,348 launches with 1-2 launches per height group (or fewer), reducing CPU overhead by 1-2 orders of magnitude.\n\n#### Opportunity 2: Fuse Fill-Zero with First Kernel\n\nCurrently, `fill_zero()` launches a separate kernel. For atomic accumulators that are reused, this could be fused into the main kernel with proper initialization.\n\n#### Opportunity 3: Batch H2D Copies\n\nInstead of copying eq_ub per window, batch all eq_ub copies for a height group before the kernel launch.\n\n#### Opportunity 4: Async H2D/D2H with Kernel Overlap\n\nUse CUDA streams and async copies to overlap:\n- H2D for window i+1\n- Kernel execution for window i\n- D2H for window i (from previous iteration)\n\n---\n\n### 7. Non-Degenerate Kernel Path\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`, Lines 228-302\n\nThis path is taken when `log_height >= l_skip + round`. It has:\n- Multiple threads processing Y-dimension coordinates\n- Windowed striding in the X dimension\n- Automatic tuning of `grid.y` (window stride) based on SM count\n\n**Launch configuration:**\n```cuda\nauto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(num_y, 256);\n// Auto-tuned window stride (grid.y) based on occupancy targets\nuint32_t tuned_stride = ...;\ngrid.y = tuned_stride;\n```\n\nThis kernel is more flexible for larger problems but still has the same per-window launch overhead.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Summary: Data Dependencies and Merging Feasibility\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Window Independence** | **Yes** — Each window accesses disjoint column ranges. Zero data dependency between windows. |\n| **Scalars (eq_r, k_rot_r)** | Same per height-group. Could broadcast to all windows of same height in one kernel. |\n| **Q matrices** | Pre-computed, static. Passed as device pointers (already optimal). |\n| **eq_ub values** | Per-column, varies per window. Could batch H2D or pre-compute. |\n| **Accumulators** | Currently one per window. Could use 2D array `accum[height_group][S_DEG * D_EF]`. |\n| **Merging Feasibility** | **High** — 10,348 launches could likely reduce to ~100-1000 launches by grouping windows by height. |\n\n---\n\n### 9. Code Flow Summary\n\n**Main entry point:** `prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu()` (Line 185)\n\n**Round 0:** `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly()` (Line 484)\n- Calls: `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0()` kernel once per trace\n- Round 0 does not use degenerate kernels\n\n**Rounds 1..n_stack:** `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` (Line 793) — **THE BOTTLENECK**\n- Loop over `ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)` — One iteration per height-group (window)\n- Decision: degenerate or non-degenerate path based on `log_height < l_skip + round`\n- **Degenerate path (lines 848-878):** Launches one tiny kernel per window\n  - This is where 10,348 launches occur at APC 300\n- Each window launch includes H2D/D2H copies around the kernel\n\n**Folding:** `fold_mle_evals()` (Line 914)\n- Folds Q matrices and eq/k_rot polynomials between rounds\n\n---\n\n### 10. Visualization of the Problem\n\n```\nTimeline at APC 300 (simplified, 10,348 windows):\n\nRound 1:\n  Window 0: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┐\n  Window 1: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┼─ 310ms wall-clock\n  Window 2: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┤   93ms actual GPU work\n  ...\n  Window 10347: fill_zero → H2D(eq_ub) → Launch kernel(1) → D2H(accum) ──┘\n                     ↑                        ↑                  ↑\n                Overhead              Overhead (3.9μs avg)    Overhead\n\nPotential Optimization:\n\nRound 1:\n  Batch H2D(all eq_ub) ──┐\n  Launch kernel(N_windows) with grid.y=N_windows ──┼─ ~10-30ms\n  Batch D2H(all accum) ──┘\n                    ↑ 10-100x speedup in CPU overhead\n```\n\n---\n\n### Files Summary\n\n| File Path | Key Content | Lines |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` | Orchestration, kernel launch loop | 793-912 |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` | Degenerate kernel def | 306-368 |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` | Degenerate kernel launcher | 537-570 |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs` | Rust FFI wrapper | 245-273 |"}],"totalDurationMs":66494,"totalTokens":62581,"totalToolUseCount":15,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":589,"cache_read_input_tokens":57257,"output_tokens":4730,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":589},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":5,"output_tokens":4730,"cache_read_input_tokens":57257,"cache_creation_input_tokens":589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":589,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standa