{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014JYkFjMUL8ouaAKxFzXJk7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task: Defer Round 0 D2H Synchronization\n2\t\n3\tTask name: 2026-04-10-1933-defer-round0-d2h-sync\n4\t\n5\t## Problem Statement\n6\t\n7\tRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase is the single largest contributor to the GPU prover's failure to scale with APC count. As APCs increase from 0 to 300, AIR instances grow from 99 to 623, but Round 0 time *increases* from 178ms to 663ms (3.7x worse), despite total computational work (cells, constraint instances, bus interaction messages) all decreasing by >2x.\n8\t\n9\tProfiling data from nsight shows that APC 300 launches ~935 Round 0 kernels (`zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_*` + `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_*`), each followed by a **blocking D2H transfer and CPU-side processing** before the next kernel can launch. This sequential pattern is in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`.\n10\t\n11\tThe per-AIR loop structure is:\n12\t```\n13\tfor each AIR instance (623 total for APC 300):\n14\t    1. Launch zerocheck kernel on GPU\n15\t    2. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 792)\n16\t    3. CPU-side IDFT computation\n17\t    4. Launch logup kernel on GPU\n18\t    5. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 848)\n19\t    6. CPU-side polynomial construction\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tThis creates 1,246 blocking synchronization points. Between each sync, the GPU sits idle while the CPU processes results. The result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each — just a few extension field elements per coset evaluation), so the overhead comes entirely from the synchronization and pipeline stalls, not from data transfer volume.\n23\t\n24\tCritically, **all loop iterations are independent**: the zerocheck and logup kernels for AIR[i] do not depend on results from any other AIR. The only shared read-only data are `d_lambda_pows` and `eq_xi_tree`.\n25\t\n26\t## Proposed Approach\n27\t\n28\tRestructure the Round 0 per-AIR loop in `LogupZerocheckGpu::batch_constraints_round0()` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`) into two phases:\n29\t\n30\t**Phase 1 — GPU Launch Phase**: Iterate over all AIR instances, launching both zerocheck and logup kernels for each, without any intermediate D2H transfers. Collect the GPU result buffers (`DeviceBuffer`) into vectors. The kernels execute sequentially on the default CUDA stream (which is correct and safe), but the CPU doesn't block waiting for each one.\n31\t\n32\t```rust\n33\tlet mut zc_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<EF>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\n34\tlet mut logup_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<FracExt>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\n35\t\n36\tfor (trace_idx, ...) in ctx.per_trace... {\n37\t    // Same setup as before (single_pk, d_main_parts, etc.)\n38\t    \n39\t    let sum_buffer = evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu(...)?;\n40\t    zc_results.push((trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...));\n41\t    \n42\t    let sum = evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu(...)?;\n43\t    logup_results.push((trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...));\n44\t}\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t**Phase 2 — CPU Processing Phase**: After all kernels are queued, process all results. The first `to_host()` call will implicitly synchronize the stream (waiting for all queued kernels to complete), and subsequent `to_host()` calls will be instant since the data is already computed.\n48\t\n49\t```rust\n50\tfor (trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...) in zc_results {\n51\t    if !sum_buffer.is_empty() {\n52\t        let q_evals = sum_buffer.to_host()?;\n53\t        // CPU IDFT and polynomial construction (unchanged)\n54\t        batch_sp_poly[2 * num_present_airs + trace_idx] = ...;\n55\t    }\n56\t}\n57\t\n58\tfor (trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...) in logup_results {\n59\t    if !sum.is_empty() {\n60\t        let evals = sum.to_host()?;\n61\t        // CPU polynomial construction (unchanged)\n62\t        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx] = ...;\n63\t        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx + 1] = ...;\n64\t    }\n65\t}\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tKey implementation details:\n69\t- `d_main_parts` (per-AIR GPU pointer arrays) must be collected to outlive Phase 1\n70\t- Result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each), so holding 623 simultaneously uses negligible GPU memory (~600KB total)\n71\t- No CUDA kernel changes are needed — only Rust-side loop restructuring\n72\t- The GPU still executes kernels sequentially on the default stream, but without host-side pipeline stalls between launches\n73\t\n74\tOptionally, the CPU processing in Phase 2 can be parallelized using rayon (`par_iter`) since each AIR's IDFT is independent, further reducing CPU-side time.\n75\t\n76\t## Expected Outcome\n77\t\n78\t- **APC 300 Round 0**: 663ms -> ~350-450ms (35-47% reduction)\n79\t  - Eliminating 1,246 sync points removes ~200-300ms of pipeline stall overhead\n80\t  - Optionally parallelizing CPU IDFTs with rayon saves additional ~50-100ms\n81\t- **APC 0 Round 0**: 178ms -> ~130-160ms (10-27% reduction, less impact due to fewer instances)\n82\t- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: 2478ms -> ~2180-2280ms (8-12% improvement)\n83\t- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace): improves from 1.15 toward ~1.05\n84\t\n85\tThis is the first step toward proper scaling. Subsequent optimizations (stacked reduction batching, multi-stream pipelining) can compound on this improvement.\n86\t\n87\t## Success Criteria\n88\t\n89\t1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace for APC 300 decreases by at least 150ms (from 2478ms baseline)\n90\t2. **Secondary**: Round 0 time for APC 300 decreases by at least 30% (from 663ms to <465ms)\n91\t3. **Scaling**: The ratio (APC 300 STARK excl trace) / (APC 0 STARK excl trace) improves (currently 1.15, target < 1.10)\n92\t4. **Correctness**: All existing tests pass, proof verification succeeds for all APC configurations\n93\t\n94\t## Considered Alternatives\n95\t\n96\t1. **Batch stacked reduction degenerate kernels** (ranked #2): Replace 10,348 single-block degenerate kernel launches with ~17 batched launches (one per round). Expected ~100-150ms savings on Stacked Reduction (0.31s -> ~0.16-0.20s). This is a good follow-up task but has lower impact (targets +195ms anti-scaling vs Round 0's +485ms) and requires CUDA kernel changes.\n97\t\n98\t2. **Multi-stream Round 0 with CPU/GPU overlap** (ranked #3): Use multiple CUDA streams to overlap GPU execution with CPU IDFT processing. Higher potential (~300-400ms savings) but significantly more complex — requires async D2H transfers, stream management, and event-based synchronization. Better suited as a follow-up after the simpler deferred-sync approach proves the benefit.\n99\t\n100\t3. **Fuse Round 0 kernels across AIR instances** (ranked #4): Launch a single large kernel that processes multiple AIRs simultaneously. High potential but difficult because each AIR has different constraint structures (different proving keys, different numbers of constraints and interactions). The data-driven kernel would be complex to implement correctly.\n101\t\n102\t4. **Parallelize CPU IDFT with rayon** (ranked #5 standalone, but can be combined with #1): Wrap the CPU-side IDFT processing in `rayon::par_iter`. Simple and effective (~50-100ms savings) but smaller standalone impact. Included as an optional enhancement in the proposed approach.\n103\t\n104\tThe deferred D2H sync approach was chosen over these alternatives because it provides the highest (impact x probability / complexity) ratio: it targets the largest bottleneck (+485ms), requires only Rust-side changes (no CUDA kernel modifications), has minimal risk (loop iterations are provably independent), and establishes the foundation for future streaming optimizations.\n105\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8b3f9a5-d318-4292-b8d9-cc11d3b0ac84","uuid":"17cd366b-78cd-41a9-88e2-1d81bfd32be9","timestamp":"2026-04-10T18:07:57.375Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Defer Round 0 D2H Synchronization\n\nTask name: 2026-04-10-1933-defer-round0-d2h-sync\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase is the single largest contributor to the GPU prover's failure to scale with APC count. As APCs increase from 0 to 300, AIR instances grow from 99 to 623, but Round 0 time *increases* from 178ms to 663ms (3.7x worse), despite total computational work (cells, constraint instances, bus interaction messages) all decreasing by >2x.\n\nProfiling data from nsight shows that APC 300 launches ~935 Round 0 kernels (`zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_*` + `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_*`), each followed by a **blocking D2H transfer and CPU-side processing** before the next kernel can launch. This sequential pattern is in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`.\n\nThe per-AIR loop structure is:\n```\nfor each AIR instance (623 total for APC 300):\n    1. Launch zerocheck kernel on GPU\n    2. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 792)\n    3. CPU-side IDFT computation\n    4. Launch logup kernel on GPU\n    5. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 848)\n    6. CPU-side polynomial construction\n```\n\nThis creates 1,246 blocking synchronization points. Between each sync, the GPU sits idle while the CPU processes results. The result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each — just a few extension field elements per coset evaluation), so the overhead comes entirely from the synchronization and pipeline stalls, not from data transfer volume.\n\nCritically, **all loop iterations are independent**: the zerocheck and logup kernels for AIR[i] do not depend on results from any other AIR. The only shared read-only data are `d_lambda_pows` and `eq_xi_tree`.\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nRestructure the Round 0 per-AIR loop in `LogupZerocheckGpu::batch_constraints_round0()` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`) into two phases:\n\n**Phase 1 — GPU Launch Phase**: Iterate over all AIR instances, launching both zerocheck and logup kernels for each, without any intermediate D2H transfers. Collect the GPU result buffers (`DeviceBuffer`) into vectors. The kernels execute sequentially on the default CUDA stream (which is correct and safe), but the CPU doesn't block waiting for each one.\n\n```rust\nlet mut zc_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<EF>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\nlet mut logup_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<FracExt>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\n\nfor (trace_idx, ...) in ctx.per_trace... {\n    // Same setup as before (single_pk, d_main_parts, etc.)\n    \n    let sum_buffer = evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu(...)?;\n    zc_results.push((trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...));\n    \n    let sum = evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu(...)?;\n    logup_results.push((trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...));\n}\n```\n\n**Phase 2 — CPU Processing Phase**: After all kernels are queued, process all results. The first `to_host()` call will implicitly synchronize the stream (waiting for all queued kernels to complete), and subsequent `to_host()` calls will be instant since the data is already computed.\n\n```rust\nfor (trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...) in zc_results {\n    if !sum_buffer.is_empty() {\n        let q_evals = sum_buffer.to_host()?;\n        // CPU IDFT and polynomial construction (unchanged)\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * num_present_airs + trace_idx] = ...;\n    }\n}\n\nfor (trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...) in logup_results {\n    if !sum.is_empty() {\n        let evals = sum.to_host()?;\n        // CPU polynomial construction (unchanged)\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx] = ...;\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx + 1] = ...;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey implementation details:\n- `d_main_parts` (per-AIR GPU pointer arrays) must be collected to outlive Phase 1\n- Result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each), so holding 623 simultaneously uses negligible GPU memory (~600KB total)\n- No CUDA kernel changes are needed — only Rust-side loop restructuring\n- The GPU still executes kernels sequentially on the default stream, but without host-side pipeline stalls between launches\n\nOptionally, the CPU processing in Phase 2 can be parallelized using rayon (`par_iter`) since each AIR's IDFT is independent, further reducing CPU-side time.\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **APC 300 Round 0**: 663ms -> ~350-450ms (35-47% reduction)\n  - Eliminating 1,246 sync points removes ~200-300ms of pipeline stall overhead\n  - Optionally parallelizing CPU IDFTs with rayon saves additional ~50-100ms\n- **APC 0 Round 0**: 178ms -> ~130-160ms (10-27% reduction, less impact due to fewer instances)\n- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: 2478ms -> ~2180-2280ms (8-12% improvement)\n- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace): improves from 1.15 toward ~1.05\n\nThis is the first step toward proper scaling. Subsequent optimizations (stacked reduction batching, multi-stream pipelining) can compound on this improvement.\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace for APC 300 decreases by at least 150ms (from 2478ms baseline)\n2. **Secondary**: Round 0 time for APC 300 decreases by at least 30% (from 663ms to <465ms)\n3. **Scaling**: The ratio (APC 300 STARK excl trace) / (APC 0 STARK excl trace) improves (currently 1.15, target < 1.10)\n4. **Correctness**: All existing tests pass, proof verification succeeds for all APC configurations\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Batch stacked reduction degenerate kernels** (ranked #2): Replace 10,348 single-block degenerate kernel launches with ~17 batched launches (one per round). Expected ~100-150ms savings on Stacked Reduction (0.31s -> ~0.16-0.20s). This is a good follow-up task but has lower impact (targets +195ms anti-scaling vs Round 0's +485ms) and requires CUDA kernel changes.\n\n2. **Multi-stream Round 0 with CPU/GPU overlap** (ranked #3): Use multiple CUDA streams to overlap GPU execution with CPU IDFT processing. Higher potential (~300-400ms savings) but significantly more complex — requires async D2H transfers, stream management, and event-based synchronization. Better suited as a follow-up after the simpler deferred-sync approach proves the benefit.\n\n3. **Fuse Round 0 kernels across AIR instances** (ranked #4): Launch a single large kernel that processes multiple AIRs simultaneously. High potential but difficult because each AIR has different constraint structures (different proving keys, different numbers of constraints and interactions). The data-driven kernel would be complex to implement correctly.\n\n4. **Parallelize CPU IDFT with rayon** (ranked #5 standalone, but can be combined with #1): Wrap the CPU-side IDFT processing in `rayon::par_iter`. Simple and effective (~50-100ms savings) but smaller standalone impact. Included as an optional enhancement in the proposed approach.\n\nThe deferred D2H sync approach was chosen over these alternatives because it provides the highest (impact x probability / complexity) ratio: it targets the largest bottleneck (+485ms), requires only Rust-side changes (no CUDA kernel modifications), has minimal risk (loop iterations are provably independent), and establishes the foundation for future streaming optimizations.\n","numLines":105,"startLine":1,"totalLines":105}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YcEH91f3sVDv7GxLRcqkDF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JAGVuxzzDMdXfi4jaT2rUc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Batch Degenerate Stacked Reduction Kernel Launches\n\nTask name: 2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe Stacked Reduction component of the GPU prover scales poorly with APC count: 113ms at APC 0 vs 310ms at APC 300 (2.74x increase), despite total work (cells, constraints, bus messages) decreasing by >2x. This is the second-largest contributor to poor scaling after Round 0 (addressed in the previous task).\n\n**Root cause**: The `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:793-912` iterates over \"windows\" (one per trace matrix), launching a separate kernel per window. For the degenerate case (`log_height < l_skip + round`), this produces **10,348 single-block kernel launches** at APC 300 (vs 3,281 at APC 0).\n\nEach degenerate window launch involves:\n1. `d_accum.fill_zero()` — a separate memset kernel\n2. H2D copy of `eq_ub_slice` (~1-2us)\n3. Degenerate kernel launch with `grid(1), block(min(window_len, 256))` — avg 3.9us GPU time\n4. `d_accum.to_host()` — D2H copy + implicit sync\n\nNsight profiling confirms: GPU kernel time for all stacked reduction kernels at APC 300 is ~93ms, but wall-clock is 310ms. **~217ms (70%) is CPU-side launch/sync overhead** from issuing 10,348+ tiny kernel launches sequentially.\n\nKey code locations:\n- Rust loop: `stacked_reduction.rs:833-907` (the `for window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)` loop)\n- CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction.cu:306-368` (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`)\n- CUDA launcher: `stacked_reduction.cu:537-570` (launches with `grid(1)`)\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nBatch all degenerate windows within each call to `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` into a single kernel launch. This is safe because:\n- Windows are independent (no data dependencies between them)\n- All window results are summed at the end (line 909-911: `s_evals_batch.iter().map(|evals| evals[i]).sum()`)\n- The kernel already uses atomic accumulation (`atomic_add_fpext_to_u64`)\n- Overflow is not a risk: 10,348 blocks x ~2^31 max per component < 2^45 << 2^64\n\n**Implementation plan:**\n\n1. **New batched CUDA kernel** (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_batched_kernel`):\n   - Grid: `(num_degenerate_windows)` — one block per window\n   - Each block loads per-window metadata from a device array: window offset into `unstacked_cols`/`lambda_pows`, window length, `eq_r`, `k_rot_r`\n   - Per-thread computation is identical to the current kernel\n   - All blocks atomically add to a single shared accumulator\n\n2. **Rust-side changes** in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()`:\n   - First pass: separate degenerate vs non-degenerate windows\n   - Degenerate path: batch all eq_ub values into one H2D copy, build per-window metadata, launch single batched kernel, single D2H copy\n   - Non-degenerate path: keep existing per-window launch (these are larger and already auto-tuned)\n\n3. **Data layout for batched kernel**:\n   - `unstacked_cols` and `lambda_pows` are already contiguous on device — just pass window offsets\n   - Pre-upload concatenated `eq_ub` values in one H2D transfer\n   - Per-window metadata struct: `{ offset: u32, len: u32, eq_r: FpExt, k_rot_r: FpExt }`\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **Stacked Reduction APC 300**: ~310ms -> ~110-130ms (save ~180-200ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 kernel launches -> 1 launch per round (save ~100-150ms of CPU launch overhead)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 fill_zero calls -> 1 memset (save ~10ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 D2H copies -> 1 D2H (save ~10-20ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 H2D eq_ub copies -> 1 H2D (save ~10-20ms)\n- **Stacked Reduction APC 0**: Minimal change (~113ms, only 3,281 launches — less overhead to begin with)\n- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: ~2358ms -> ~2160-2180ms (cumulative with previous task: ~300ms vs baseline)\n- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0): ~1.086 -> ~1.00-1.02 (near parity)\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: Stacked Reduction APC 300 drops below 150ms (>50% reduction from 310ms)\n2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 drops below 2200ms (>10% reduction from baseline 2478ms)\n3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio drops below 1.05\n4. **Correctness**: All `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Rayon parallelization of Round 0 Phase 2** (~30-50ms savings at APC 300, High probability, Simple complexity). Chosen against because: lower absolute impact than Stacked Reduction batching. Good follow-up task.\n\n2. **Multi-stream pipelining for Round 0** (~50-100ms savings, Medium probability, Complex). Chosen against because: complex synchronization with uncertain overlap benefit. Higher risk.\n\n3. **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels** (~30-50ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: non-degenerate kernels already do more work per launch (auto-tuned grid.y), so per-launch overhead is proportionally smaller. Could be added as an enhancement within this task if the degenerate batching succeeds.\n\n4. **Batch MLE round kernel launches** (~20-30ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: smaller absolute impact than Stacked Reduction. Less clear batching opportunity.\n\nStacked Reduction batching was selected because it has the highest (expected impact x probability / complexity): ~180ms savings with high confidence (windows are provably independent, all results are summed) and moderate implementation effort (new kernel + loop restructuring, following the proven pattern from the Round 0 optimization).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2391,"cache_read_input_tokens":87559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2391},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8b3f9a5-d318-4292-b8d9-cc11d3b0ac84","uuid":"892c4873-f9bf-4ea3-81ea-90d45a253aa8"}
